# Vue Options API — Core Principles

**Official Vue 3 docs via Context7:** use MCP `query-docs` with library ID `/vuejs/docs` ([vuejs/docs on Context7](https://context7.com/vuejs/docs)). Scope questions to the **Options API**. See `docs/VUE_CONTEXT7.md` in the repo for the full note.

---

## Data Flow Lifecycle

Data is always fetched **before** the component renders. Follow this order strictly:

1. **`asyncData` / server prefetch** — fetch data that must exist on load (SSR or route-level)
2. **`created`** — fetch data that can start immediately (no DOM needed)
3. **`mounted`** — only if DOM or browser APIs are required

Never fetch inside methods unless triggered by a user action (button click, form submit, etc.).

```js
// ✅ data fetched in created — available before first render
created() {
  this.loadPageData();
},
methods: {
  loadPageData() {
    Promise.all([this.fetchUsers(), this.fetchStats()]).then(this.applyPageData);
  },
}
```

---

## Separation of Concerns in Methods

Each method does **one thing**. Split by responsibility, not by convenience.

**Two API calls = two fetch methods:**

```js
methods: {
  async fetchUsers() { ... },
  async fetchStats() { ... },
}
```

**Mapping a structure for display = a separate transform method:**

```js
// ✅ receives raw data, returns display-ready structure
getTableRows(rawData) { ... },
getCards(rawData) { ... },
```

**Helpers used only inside one method = local `const` inside that method:**

```js
getTableRows(rawData) {
  const toLabel = (item) => item.name || item.id;
  const toRow = (item, i) => ({ index: i + 1, label: toLabel(item) });
  return rawData.map(toRow);
},
```

**Helpers reused across methods = extract as a component method, call via `this`:**

```js
formatCurrency(n) {
  return `${n.toFixed(2)} ₴`;
},
getTableRows(rawData) {
  const toRow = (item) => ({ price: this.formatCurrency(item.price) });
  return rawData.map(toRow);
},
```

---

## What Lives Between `<script>` and `export default`

Only **imports** and **constants**. No function declarations. No logic.

```js
// ✅ allowed
import { formatDate } from '@908inc/core';

const API_USERS = '/api/users';
const EMPTY_STATE = { users: [], total: 0 };
const TABLE_FIELDS = [
  { key: 'name', label: 'Імʼя' },
  { key: 'date', label: 'Дата' },
];
```

**Imported functions are used directly — no wrapper methods needed:**

```js
// ✅ use imported function directly inside a method
getTableRows(rawData) {
  const toRow = (item) => ({ date: formatDate(item.createdAt) });
  return rawData.map(toRow);
},
```

**If an imported function is called from template, expose it as a method:**

```js
// template calls: {{ formatDate(row.date) }}
methods: {
  formatDate,  // ✅ re-export as method for template access
}
```

---

## Template Calls Methods — Methods Do the Work

Template renders results. JavaScript computes them.

```pug
//- ✅ template calls method with raw data, gets ready-to-render result
OdbTable(:items='getTableRows(rawUsers)')

v-for= 'card in getCards(rawCars)'
  Cards(:item='card')
```

- No expressions inside template (no `.filter()`, `.map()`, ternaries for display logic)
- All method parameters come **from the outside** — passed as arguments, never read from `this` inside the method body
- `this` inside methods is only for calling other methods or accessing reactive `data`

---

## No `computed` Unless Explicitly Needed

Default to `methods`. Use `computed` only when reactivity to multiple sources is genuinely required — and only when asked or clearly necessary.

```js
// ❌ computed for a simple transform
computed: {
  formattedTitle() { return this.title.toUpperCase(); }
}

// ✅ method instead
formatTitle(title) { return title.toUpperCase(); }
```

**Exception — multi-step wizards:** Dense flows (e.g. IBAN → bank → credentials) may keep **chained `computed`** properties for derived step state, bank key, progress, and gating flags so each piece stays a named concept and templates stay thin. Prefer **methods** for anything invoked explicitly from the template with arguments (IDs, labels, keys). This does not override the **Template Calls Methods** rule for string concat and display ternaries in Pug.

---

## Pages vs Components

**Pages** know the context. They own the lifecycle, fetch raw data, and map it to abstract shapes.

**Components** are isolated. They render abstract fields — `title`, `subtitle`, `description`, `value`, `label`. They know nothing about domain.

```
❌  HumanCards.vue, CarCards.vue
✅  Cards.vue  — renders whatever { title, subtitle, description } it receives
```

**The full flow in a page:**

```js
// 1. fetch raw domain data in created
created() {
  this.rawCars = this.fetchCars()
},

// 2. map domain fields → abstract card shape
getCards(cars) {
  const toCard = (car) => ({
    title: car.carName,
    subtitle: car.brand,
    description: car.year,
  });
  return cars.map(toCard);
},
```

```pug
//- 3. template passes the whole abstract object — component decides what to render
div(v-for='card in getCards(rawCars)', :key='card.title')
  Cards(:item='card')
```

The component `Cards.vue` has no idea what a "car" is. It receives an abstract `item` and decides itself which fields to render.

---

## Summary: The Cycle

```
asyncData/created → fetch rawData
       ↓
methods → transform rawData into display-ready shapes (getCards, getTableRows, etc.)
       ↓
template → call methods with rawData, pass results to abstract components
       ↓
component → renders abstract fields, knows nothing about domain
```
